Part 1: Set Up the Two NAT Gateways (Run Once)

Spin up two minimal Ubuntu/Debian VPSs inside the same private VPC/network as your ADT webservers, each with a static Reserved/Elastic public IP. Whitelist both public IPs in your DBaaS GUI once.
Run this setup script on both Gateway 1 and Gateway 2 (adjust eth0 if your public interface has a different name, and 10.0.0.0/16 to match your VPC subnet):
bash
#!/bin/bash
set -e

PUBLIC_IF="eth0" (this might be different on ubuntu and debian)
VPC_CIDR="10.0.0.0/16"

# 1. Enable IPv4 forwarding permanently
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" > /etc/sysctl.d/99-nat-gateway.conf
sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-nat-gateway.conf

# 2. Configure NAT masquerading
iptables -t nat -F POSTROUTING
iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s "${VPC_CIDR}" -o "${PUBLIC_IF}" -j MASQUERADE

# 3. Persist iptables rules across reboots
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get update -qq
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y -qq iptables-persistent
netfilter-persistent save
Use code with caution.
(Note: Ensure your cloud firewall/security group allows internal VPC traffic to reach both gateways, and lock down SSH on both gateways to your admin IP.)

Part 2: Webserver Route & Failover Daemon (Add to ADT Webserver Build)

Drop this script into /usr/local/sbin/dbaas-route-failover.sh during your ADT webserver build. It handles both gateway health failover (every 3 seconds) and dynamic DNS resolution (for endpoints like AWS Aurora whose IP addresses can change during DB failover).
bash
#!/bin/bash
# /usr/local/sbin/dbaas-route-failover.sh

DB_HOST="<YOUR_DBAAS_HOSTNAME_OR_IP>"
GW_PRIMARY="10.0.0.10"
GW_BACKUP="10.0.0.11"
STATE_FILE="/run/dbaas_active_routes"

touch "${STATE_FILE}"

while true; do
    # 1. Determine healthy gateway (prefer Primary, fall back to Backup)
    if ping -c 1 -W 1 "${GW_PRIMARY}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        ACTIVE_GW="${GW_PRIMARY}"
    elif ping -c 1 -W 1 "${GW_BACKUP}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        ACTIVE_GW="${GW_BACKUP}"
    else
        sleep 2
        continue
    fi

    # 2. Resolve DB hostname to current IPv4 address(es) (works for static IPs too)
    if [[ "${DB_HOST}" =~ ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        CURRENT_IPS="${DB_HOST}"
    else
        CURRENT_IPS=$(getent ahostsv4 "${DB_HOST}" | awk '{print $1}' | sort -u)
    fi

    # 3. Apply/update routes if IPs or active gateway changed
    DESIRED_STATE=""
    for IP in ${CURRENT_IPS}; do
        ip route replace "${IP}/32" via "${ACTIVE_GW}"
        DESIRED_STATE+="${IP}:${ACTIVE_GW}"$'\n'
    done

    # 4. Clean up stale routes if Aurora/DBaaS DNS rotated to a new IP
    while IFS=: read -r OLD_IP OLD_GW; do
        if [[ -n "${OLD_IP}" ]] && ! echo "${CURRENT_IPS}" | grep -qx "${OLD_IP}"; then
            ip route del "${OLD_IP}/32" 2>/dev/null || true
        fi
    done < "${STATE_FILE}"

    printf "%s" "${DESIRED_STATE}" > "${STATE_FILE}"
    sleep 3
done
Use code with caution.
Make it executable and register it as a systemd service so it starts automatically on boot and restarts if interrupted:
bash
chmod +x /usr/local/sbin/dbaas-route-failover.sh

cat << 'EOF' > /etc/systemd/system/dbaas-route-failover.service
[Unit]
Description=DBaaS Dual-Gateway Route Failover
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/dbaas-route-failover.sh
Restart=always
RestartSec=2

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now dbaas-route-failover.service
Use code with caution.

Part 3: Minimal Changes to Wintersys ADT

To integrate this cleanly with your "GUI + Template + SSL Cert" plan:
1. Add 4 template variables to your ADT build template:bash
CUSTOM_DBAAS="1"
DB_GATEWAY_PRIMARY="10.0.0.10"
DB_GATEWAY_BACKUP="10.0.0.11"
DBAAS_SSL_CERT_B64="<BASE64_ENCODED_CA_PEM>"
Use code with caution.
2. Skip ADT's firewall API call: At the very top of AdjustDBaaSFirewall.sh in adt-build-machine-scripts, add:bash
if [ "${CUSTOM_DBAAS}" = "1" ]; then
    exit 0
fi
Use code with caution.
3. Inject the SSL cert & start the route service on webserver boot:
	• Decode ${DBAAS_SSL_CERT_B64} to /etc/ssl/certs/dbaas-ca.pem (chmod 644).
	• Write ${DB_HOST}, ${DB_GATEWAY_PRIMARY}, and ${DB_GATEWAY_BACKUP} into /usr/local/sbin/dbaas-route-failover.sh and start dbaas-route-failover.service before ADT runs its database connectivity check or CMS installation.
4. Configure SSL in your CMS & backup scripts:
	• Point your CMS database connection settings (e.g., MYSQL_SSL_CA / sslmode=verify-full) and adt-database-scripts (--ssl-ca=/etc/ssl/certs/dbaas-ca.pem) to /etc/ssl/certs/dbaas-ca.pem.

Why This Hits Every Requirement

• Zero API keys or firewall calls: ADT never talks to cloud firewall APIs.
• No single point of failure: If Gateway 1 dies or reboots, webservers reroute through Gateway 2 within 3 seconds.
• Handles Aurora DNS failover: If AWS Aurora fails over to another AZ and changes its endpoint IP, getent ahostsv4 picks up the new IP and updates the route within 3 seconds.
• 100% provider-agnostic: Works identically on DigitalOcean, Linode, Vultr, and Exoscale.
