What You Do for Each Region

• 2 small NAT Gateways per webserver region: For example, if you deploy webservers in London and Frankfurt, spin up 2 small NAT Gateways in London (2 static IPs) and 2 small NAT Gateways in Frankfurt (2 static IPs).
• Whitelist all 4 static IPs once in the DBaaS GUI: Your database firewall simply allows those 4 static IPv4 addresses—zero dynamic API updates regardless of how many webservers scale up or down in either region.
• Keep DB_GATEWAY_PRIMARY and DB_GATEWAY_BACKUP per-region in your ADT templates: Each region's ADT build template already has its own config file, so you simply put that region's two private gateway IPs into its template.
(Note: If you later use AWS Aurora Global Database where Frankfurt webservers connect to a local Frankfurt Aurora endpoint and London webservers connect to a London Aurora endpoint, this exact same setup still works out of the box—each region's dbaas-route-failover.sh simply resolves its own template's DB_HOST and routes it through its local regional NAT Gateways.)
